{
  "dom_id": "step-t1404",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We can use unit conversions to convert from one set of units to another.\n\nFor instance, the speed of sound is approximately $0.34$ kilometers per second. What is this in meters per second? We can find out using the following two steps.\n\n** Step 1: Create a conversion formula.**\n\nWe are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{km}$ with $\\textrm{m}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{km},$ we can put $\\textrm{km}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}} }{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{?}{\\cancel{ \\textrm{km} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThen, to introduce $\\textrm{m},$ we can put $\\textrm{m}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}} }{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m} }{\\cancel{ \\textrm{km} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{s}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m} }{\\textrm{km} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Use the conversion formula.**\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed that we wish to convert is $0.34 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{s}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.34\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}} & =0.34\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =(0.34\\cdot 1\\,000)\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =340\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of sound is $340$ meters per second.",
